# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: clautolisp-issue-on-error -- an error in a -l file before -i ends the program; --on-error ignore does not continue
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-11-03

* Report (pjb, cecil, 2026-11-03)

#+begin_example
18:06[pjb@cecil :1 clautolisp 3.4T]$ clautolisp -norc --on-error ignore -l ~/.autolisp --on-error debug -i
DBG> clautolisp debugger attached (h for help)
DBG> detached
clautolisp: runtime error: LOAD-FILE-NOT-FOUND: LOAD could not locate /Users/pjb/works/sncf-reseau/src/outils-autolisp/autolisp-misc/src/browser.lsp.
AutoLISP backtrace (most recent call first, 4 frames):
  in SUBR LOAD: ("/Users/pjb/works/sncf-reseau/src/outils-autolisp/autolisp-misc/src/browser.lsp")
  in EVAL: (LOAD "/Users/pjb/works/sncf-reseau/src/outils-autolisp/autolisp-misc/src/browser.lsp")
  in USUBR LL-MISC: ()
  in EVAL: (LL-MISC)
master
#+end_example

* Analysis

Reproduced with 2.3.81 on macOS (=rc.lsp= calling =(load "/nonexistent/...")=):
whatever the =--on-error= policy, the error in the =-l= file ended the
program with status 1 before the REPL asked for by =-i= started
("DBG> detached" is the debugger session being torn down on the way out).

- RUN-WITH-INPUT ran the actions before the REPL PLAIN ("init files are
  machinery you don't debug"), so an uncaught error there escaped to the
  program's outer handler, which reports and exits.
- =--on-error ignore= was documented as "continue past the error ... keep
  going to the next form / action" (debugger-public-interface-and-on-error
  B.1) but had never been implemented: in a batch run it exited like quit
  (noted, "observed, not changed", in that issue).
- =--on-error= is not positional: the last occurrence (here =debug=)
  decides for the whole run, so the =ignore= before =-l= had no effect.

* Resolved (clautolisp 2.3.86 / alfe 2.3.86, 2026-11-04)

- =CONTINUE-PAST-ACTION-ERRORS-P= (main.lisp): the run goes on past an
  action's error under =ignore=, and before an interactive REPL under any
  policy but =quit=. =RUN-ACTIONS-CONTINUING-PAST-ERRORS= reports the error
  the engine's way (AutoLISP error, or a =-l= file that cannot be opened)
  and runs the next action.
- Before the REPL, each action is its own debugging extent, as a REPL turn
  is: under =debug= the error stops in the debugger; after =q= (or =c=)
  the next action runs, then the REPL.
- A batch run that went on past an error still exits with the AutoLISP-
  error status (1), so CI does not take it for a clean run; a user
  =*error*= that takes the error leaves the status at 0, as before.
- alfe's direct variant does the same under =--on-error ignore=
  (backend-clautolisp.lisp); its parity row "on-error ignore" now expects
  "aafter", status 1, in both variants.
- Tests: CONTINUE-PAST-ACTION-ERRORS-POLICY and GUARDED-ACTIONS-CONTINUE-
  PAST-AN-ERROR (tools/clautolisp/tests/exit-status-tests.lisp); the alfe
  parity table. Checked end to end with the built clautolisp-sbcl:
  =--on-error ignore -l rc.lsp -i= reaches the REPL;
  =--on-error ignore -l rc.lsp --on-error debug -i= stops in the debugger,
  =q= =y=, then the REPL; =--on-error ignore -l rc.lsp -x ...= runs the
  =-x=, exit 1; the default batch run still exits 1 at the error.
- Docs: --help, man pages, clautolisp and alfe user manuals, alfe spec.

Not done: making =--on-error= POSITIONAL (one policy for the =-l= before
it, another for the REPL after it), which is what the report's command
line suggests. Left to pjb's decision.
